WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:03.360
Arguments are extraordinary things.

00:00:03.440 --> 00:00:05.480
Painful things sometimes,

00:00:05.560 --> 00:00:08.680
but fantastic ways
of clarifying thought.

00:00:08.760 --> 00:00:11.640
That could be arguing with your
partner, arguing with a friend,

00:00:11.720 --> 00:00:14.440
arguing in the law court,
arguing in parliament.

00:00:14.520 --> 00:00:17.840
But for me, at least,
all these types of arguments

00:00:17.920 --> 00:00:22.280
show where the truth is going wrong,
helps you to think more clearly,

00:00:22.360 --> 00:00:24.840
and in my case,
helps me to change my mind.

00:00:30.320 --> 00:00:33.480
A successful argument
basically starts

00:00:33.560 --> 00:00:37.400
with a really good
grasp of the subject.

00:00:37.480 --> 00:00:39.640
You need to choose your subject well.

00:00:39.720 --> 00:00:41.480
Secondly, structure.

00:00:42.080 --> 00:00:45.520
It needs a very clear beginning,
middle and end.

00:00:45.600 --> 00:00:49.120
Thirdly, the content of it,
the words, the phrases,

00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:52.480
the metaphors, the jokes,
the things that give it life.

00:00:53.080 --> 00:00:55.560
And finally, emotion.

00:00:56.400 --> 00:00:59.240
And the way in which that's
carried out, not just in the words, 

00:00:59.320 --> 00:01:02.480
but in your voice and in the way
that you move your body.

00:01:08.080 --> 00:01:12.520
When I'm making a speech or I'm about
to go into a formal public argument, 

00:01:13.800 --> 00:01:17.000
I firstly work out very clearly
what the structure is.

00:01:17.080 --> 00:01:20.520
What are the two points or
five points that I'm trying to land?

00:01:21.280 --> 00:01:24.160
Then I think very carefully
about memorising the first line

00:01:24.240 --> 00:01:27.840
and the last line, so that
I can finish on a strong point.

00:01:28.440 --> 00:01:30.200
And then I walk and practise.

00:01:30.280 --> 00:01:33.280
Speak and practise.
Speak and practise.

00:01:33.360 --> 00:01:36.720
 And sometimes it just flows.
Sometimes it's magical.

00:01:36.960 --> 00:01:38.080
 And sometimes you find

00:01:38.160 --> 00:01:39.360
that something you thought
was going to be magical

00:01:39.440 --> 00:01:42.840
has become all kind of tortured
and contorted and too complicated.

00:01:42.920 --> 00:01:44.680
 And it's not really working.

00:01:49.280 --> 00:01:52.840
Arguments go wrong in many,
many different ways.

00:01:53.120 --> 00:01:55.520
 One of the main ways
in which we go wrong

00:01:55.600 --> 00:01:58.360
is that we just lose control
of our words

00:01:58.440 --> 00:02:00.320
and we lose control
of our emotions.

00:02:00.920 --> 00:02:03.640
Emotion is very, very dangerous
in an argument.

00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:06.280
 It can really put people's backs up.

00:02:07.280 --> 00:02:08.920
 And really upset them.

00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:12.560
 And we feel,
at a sort of animal level,

00:02:12.640 --> 00:02:14.240
somebody’s emotion coming at us

00:02:14.320 --> 00:02:17.400
and it can make us sort of shut down
and no longer listen to the words.

00:02:17.640 --> 00:02:20.600
But at the same time,
emotion done properly

00:02:20.680 --> 00:02:24.040
is really the only way
to win an argument,

00:02:24.120 --> 00:02:26.160
because nobody's
just going to listen to words.

00:02:26.240 --> 00:02:28.800
So the master in argument

00:02:28.880 --> 00:02:31.600
is somebody
who can perfectly calibrate

00:02:31.680 --> 00:02:34.000
when to hold back
and when to go for it.

00:02:38.280 --> 00:02:41.480
The best argument
I've probably ever won

00:02:41.560 --> 00:02:43.200
was in the first leadership debate

00:02:43.280 --> 00:02:45.320
when I was running against
the other candidates

00:02:45.400 --> 00:02:46.960
to be leader
of the Conservative Party,

00:02:47.040 --> 00:02:48.280
to be prime minister of Britain.

00:02:48.520 --> 00:02:51.440
 And I suddenly found,
about halfway through the debate,

00:02:51.520 --> 00:02:55.040
that I had the right type
of comparison to use.

00:02:55.120 --> 00:02:59.960
I suddenly remembered that
I'd been trying to put three bin bags

00:03:00.040 --> 00:03:01.360
into the rubbish bin at home.

00:03:01.560 --> 00:03:02.720
And my wife said,

00:03:02.800 --> 00:03:05.480
"You're never going to get these
three huge bags of rubbish in."

00:03:05.560 --> 00:03:10.800
And I was tempted, like Michael and
like Dom to say "Believe in the bin",

00:03:10.880 --> 00:03:13.840
"Believe in Britain" right?
It's nonsense!

00:03:13.920 --> 00:03:15.840
They're not going to get
a different deal out of Europe,

00:03:15.920 --> 00:03:16.840
we all know that.

00:03:16.920 --> 00:03:18.040
 They know it themselves.

00:03:18.360 --> 00:03:21.160
I produced it and I won the audience
round and I won the debate.

00:03:26.120 --> 00:03:28.040
The worst argument I had in my life

00:03:28.120 --> 00:03:30.440
was the second big TV debate I did

00:03:30.520 --> 00:03:32.560
when I was running to be leader
of the Conservative Party,

00:03:32.640 --> 00:03:36.440
this was my big chance to prove
that I could be prime minister.

00:03:36.640 --> 00:03:39.320
 And I thought I had the
most killer argument.

00:03:39.400 --> 00:03:42.520
 And I was just going to say
to Boris Johnson,

00:03:42.600 --> 00:03:46.800
how are you going to get
Brexit done by the 31st of October?

00:03:47.960 --> 00:03:50.800
And I kept asking the question
and nobody would answer.

00:03:50.880 --> 00:03:53.200
 And I got more and more
wound up with myself.

00:03:53.280 --> 00:03:55.760
 And my whole body language
kind of cramped up.

00:03:55.840 --> 00:03:57.520
 My voice became very contorted.

00:03:57.600 --> 00:04:01.520
 I took off my tie, very bizarrely,
in the middle of this debate,

00:04:01.600 --> 00:04:06.160
and I felt
within about two paragraphs

00:04:06.240 --> 00:04:09.400
I had managed to lose
my entire vision for Britain.

00:04:09.480 --> 00:04:12.360
Tonight you were a bit lacklustre,
weren't you?

00:04:12.440 --> 00:04:15.360
I didn’t find that format
really worked for me,

00:04:15.440 --> 00:04:18.280
and I’m going to have
to learn how I flourish

00:04:18.360 --> 00:04:20.680
in the strange format
of alternative reality.

00:04:24.920 --> 00:04:27.960
One of the great changes in the
history of argument is, of course,

00:04:28.040 --> 00:04:32.360
the arrival of television, because
suddenly it's possible for people

00:04:32.440 --> 00:04:35.760
to view politicians
in particular in action.

00:04:35.840 --> 00:04:38.040
 And the great moment where
this really came into prominence

00:04:38.120 --> 00:04:42.000
was the TV debate
between Kennedy and Nixon

00:04:42.080 --> 00:04:47.200
about who was going to be president,
and famously, Nixon won on radio.

00:04:47.920 --> 00:04:50.960
His arguments were much more
serious, much more thoughtful.

00:04:51.040 --> 00:04:53.840
 But Kennedy just glowed
on television,

00:04:53.920 --> 00:04:55.720
and that's what really
won him the presidency.

00:05:06.280 --> 00:05:07.560
I love arguing on Twitter.

00:05:07.640 --> 00:05:10.880
 There's something
very, very satisfying

00:05:10.960 --> 00:05:13.520
about the discipline
of that word limit.

00:05:13.800 --> 00:05:16.040
 You get to put out your argument

00:05:16.120 --> 00:05:19.240
and then somebody comes back
and then you come back at them.

00:05:19.320 --> 00:05:21.760
 And I love the clarity of that.

00:05:21.960 --> 00:05:24.200
 It's a beautiful way of arguing,

00:05:24.280 --> 00:05:26.640
which wasn't available to us
until Twitter was around.

00:05:31.280 --> 00:05:33.920
One of the things that's very
difficult about argument

00:05:34.000 --> 00:05:34.960
is that in the modern world,

00:05:35.040 --> 00:05:38.600
we often see it as a very,
very bad thing, arguing.

00:05:38.880 --> 00:05:41.760
 Sometimes it's seen as a very
male thing, a very aggressive thing,

00:05:41.840 --> 00:05:44.240
something that makes people unhappy.

00:05:44.320 --> 00:05:45.640
 It's almost a form of bullying.

00:05:45.920 --> 00:05:51.320
 But if you don't argue, it is very,
very difficult to get to the truth.

00:05:52.200 --> 00:05:55.360
 If you just let everything slide,
if nothing gets challenged,

00:05:55.440 --> 00:05:58.680
if you don't try to really pin down
what somebody's saying,

00:05:59.480 --> 00:06:01.640
you get a very lazy form of thought.

00:06:01.720 --> 00:06:05.400
And a lazy form of thought,
in the end, is a version of lying.

